{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis tabs component implements the **ARIA attribute layer correctly** \u2014 roles, states, roving tabindex, and panel associations are all present. However, it is a textbook case of the \"80% pattern\" problem: the DOM is wired for assistive technology, but the **keyboard interaction model is absent**. A keyboard-only user who tabs into this component lands on the active tab and **cannot reach any other tab**. There is no ArrowLeft/ArrowRight handler, no Home/End handler, and the only keydown logic present (Enter/Space) is redundant with native `<button>` behavior. This is a complete access failure for a user category, not a degradation.\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, I predicted for a custom tabs widget:\n1. **Arrow key navigation missing** \u2014 *CONFIRMED. No ArrowLeft/ArrowRight handler exists.*\n2. **Home/End key support missing** \u2014 *CONFIRMED. Not handled.*\n3. **Redundant Enter/Space handling on native buttons** \u2014 *CONFIRMED. `handleTabKeyDown` intercepts keys that `<button>` already handles natively, with a `preventDefault()` that risks breaking Space activation.*\n4. **Focus management on tab switch undefined** \u2014 *PARTIALLY CONFIRMED. Click works natively; keyboard activation path is broken because there is no keyboard navigation to trigger it.*\n5. **No heading inside panels** \u2014 *CONFIRMED. Panels contain only `<p>` text.*\n\nNo surprises. Every prediction landed.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n### 1. Keyboard users cannot switch tabs \u2014 no arrow key navigation\n\n**Evidence:** `TabsWidget.jsx`, `handleTabKeyDown` (lines 10\u201315). The handler checks only for `'Enter'` and `' '`. There is no handler for `'ArrowLeft'`, `'ArrowRight'`, `'Home'`, or `'End'`. Combined with the roving tabindex (`tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}`), only the active tab is in the tab order. All other tabs are `tabindex=\"-1\"` and unreachable by Tab.\n\n**User group impacted:** Keyboard-only users, screen reader users navigating via keyboard (the standard mode for NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver).\n\n**Why this matters:** A keyboard user tabs into the component, lands on the active tab, and is **completely locked out of all other tab panels**. They cannot move focus to another tab by any means: Tab skips them (tabindex=\"-1\"), arrow keys do nothing (no handler), and there is no other keyboard path. This is not a degraded experience \u2014 it is a total access failure for the component's primary purpose.\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard):** \"All functionality of the interface is operable through a keyboard interface.\"\n- **WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern, Keyboard Interaction:** \"Arrow keys move focus between tabs. Home moves focus to the first tab. End moves focus to the last tab.\"\n\n**Fix:** Replace `handleTabKeyDown` with a proper roving-tabindex arrow-key handler:\n\n```jsx\nconst handleTabKeyDown = (e) => {\n  const currentIndex = tabs.findIndex(t => t.id === e.currentTarget.id);\n  let newIndex = null;\n\n  switch (e.key) {\n    case 'ArrowRight':\n      newIndex = (currentIndex + 1) % tabs.length;\n      break;\n    case 'ArrowLeft':\n      newIndex = (currentIndex - 1 + tabs.length) % tabs.length;\n      break;\n    case 'Home':\n      newIndex = 0;\n      break;\n    case 'End':\n      newIndex = tabs.length - 1;\n      break;\n    default:\n      return; // let native button handle Enter/Space\n  }\n\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setActiveTab(newIndex);\n  // Move focus to the newly activated tab\n  const newTabEl = document.getElementById(`tab-${tabs[newIndex].id}`);\n  newTabEl.focus();\n};\n```\n\nRemove the Enter/Space handling entirely \u2014 `<button>` handles those natively.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **Realist check:** Worst case is total loss of tab-switching for keyboard users. No workaround exists. Detection would require keyboard testing (a11y-test) or user testing \u2014 automated axe-core will NOT catch this because the ARIA attributes are all present. Severity is correctly CRITICAL.\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n### 2. Redundant and fragile Enter/Space keydown handler on native `<button>` elements\n\n**Evidence:** `TabsWidget.jsx`, lines 10\u201315:\n\n```jsx\nconst handleTabKeyDown = (e) => {\n  if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    handleTabClick(tabs.findIndex(t => t.id === e.currentTarget.id));\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis is attached to `<button>` elements via `onKeyDown={handleTabKeyDown}`.\n\n**User group impacted:** All keyboard users (theoretically), but in practice the handler works \u2014 so the impact is fragility, not current failure.\n\n**Why this matters:**\n- `<button>` natively fires `click` on Enter (keydown) and Space (keyup). The handler intercepts both, calls `e.preventDefault()`, and then manually invokes `handleTabClick`. For Enter, this is redundant. For Space, `e.preventDefault()` on keydown **prevents the native keyup click** in some browser implementations, meaning the handler's manual `handleTabClick` call is the *only* thing making Space work. If that call were removed or the handler refactored, Space activation would silently break.\n- This pattern (manually handling Enter/Space) is the standard approach for `role=\"tab\"` on a `<div>`. Applying it to a `<button>` signals the developer was thinking in div-based terms and layered ARIA on top of native semantics. The native HTML-first principle says: rely on `<button>` for activation, use `onKeyDown` only for navigation keys (arrows, Home, End).\n\n**WCAG / APG citation:**\n- **WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value):** Interactive elements must be operable. The current code works, but the mechanism is fragile.\n- **Native HTML first principle:** ARIA must not be used when equivalent semantics and behavior are available in HTML. `<button>` already provides keyboard activation.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the Enter/Space branch entirely. Let `<button>` handle activation natively via `onClick`. Use `onKeyDown` exclusively for ArrowLeft/ArrowRight/Home/End navigation (see Critical Finding #1 fix).\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **Realist check:** In practice, the handler works today. The risk is future refactoring breaking Space activation. Downgrade from MAJOR to MINOR? No \u2014 the pattern is a design smell that indicates the keyboard model was not thought through, and it directly contributes to Finding #1 (the developer handled the \"wrong\" keys and missed the \"right\" keys). Keep as MAJOR because it's evidence of an incomplete interaction model, not just a code style issue.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n### 3. No heading element inside tab panels\n\n**Evidence:** `TabsWidget.jsx`, panel rendering (lines 37\u201346). Each panel contains only `<p>{tab.content}</p>`. No `<h2>`, `<h3>`, or other heading.\n\n**User group impacted:** Screen reader users who navigate by heading (a primary navigation strategy).\n\n**Why this matters:** A screen reader user browsing the page by headings (H key in NVDA, VO+Up/Down in VoiceOver) will not encounter the tab panel content as a navigable section. The panel is reachable via the tab, but not via heading navigation.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 headings provide structure.\n\n**Fix:** Add a heading to each panel:\n```jsx\n<div role=\"tabpanel\" ...>\n  <h2 className=\"visually-hidden\">{tab.label}</h2>\n  <p>{tab.content}</p>\n</div>\n```\nOr make the first line of content a heading if the content structure supports it.\n\n- **Confidence:** MEDIUM (depends on whether the page has other heading structure that covers this)\n\n### 4. `aria-orientation` not set on tablist\n\n**Evidence:** `TabsWidget.jsx`, line 19: `<div role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Content tabs\">`. No `aria-orientation` attribute.\n\n**User group impacted:** Screen reader users (minor \u2014 most SRs default to horizontal).\n\n**Why this matters:** The APG Tabs pattern lists `aria-orientation` as an optional property. Without it, AT assumes horizontal, which is correct here. This is a best-practice gap, not a functional issue.\n\n**Fix:** Add `aria-orientation=\"horizontal\"` for explicitness.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH (it's missing, but the default is correct)\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n### 5. No `prefers-reduced-motion` guard on the color transition\n\n**Evidence:** CSS, `.tab-button`: `transition: color 0.2s;`\n\nThe 0.2s color transition is subtle and unlikely to cause vestibular issues, but a `prefers-reduced-motion` media query would be a defensive best practice.\n\n**Fix:**\n```css\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .tab-button {\n    transition: none;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 6. Focus indicator could be verified against WCAG 2.4.13 (Focus Appearance)\n\n**Evidence:** CSS, `.tab-button:focus`: `outline: 3px solid #0066cc; outline-offset: 2px;`\n\nThe 3px outline with 2px offset gives a total perimeter of 5px, which exceeds the 2px minimum. The #0066cc color against the white background has a contrast ratio of approximately 5.5:1, exceeding the 3:1 requirement. **This passes.** Noted as verified, not a finding.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)\n\n1. **Arrow key navigation (ArrowLeft, ArrowRight)** \u2014 The single most important gap. The APG Tabs pattern's core keyboard interaction is absent. This is not an enhancement; it is the primary keyboard interaction model for tabs.\n\n2. **Home/End key support** \u2014 Part of the same APG requirement. Without it, a user with 20 tabs cannot jump to the first or last tab.\n\n3. **Focus movement on tab activation via keyboard** \u2014 When a tab is activated via arrow keys, focus must move to the newly activated tab. The current code has no mechanism for this because there are no arrow keys. The fix in Critical Finding #1 includes `newTabEl.focus()`.\n\n4. **`aria-orientation`** \u2014 Optional but recommended for explicitness.\n\n5. **Heading in panels** \u2014 Screen reader heading navigation does not reach panel content.\n\n6. **No test evidence provided** \u2014 No axe-core results, no Playwright keyboard test results, no virtual-screen-reader output. The findings above are based on code reading. A keyboard test (`a11y-test` with `page.keyboard.press('ArrowRight')`) would confirm the Critical Finding with measured evidence.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Custom widget; SR users navigating by keyboard are blocked from switching tabs; no heading in panels |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Complete access failure \u2014 cannot switch tabs; only active tab is reachable |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Focus indicator passes; contrast passes; verify reflow at 200% zoom (flex layout should reflow) |\n| Cognitive | LOW | Simple interaction model; clear labels; no confusing state |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | Single 0.2s color transition; no significant motion |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | All contrast ratios pass; no color-only indicators (active state uses color + border + font-weight) |\n\n**Screen reader user experience:** Tabs are correctly labeled, selected state is announced, panels are linked. However, a screen reader user who switches to browse mode and tries to use keyboard to navigate tabs (the standard workflow) will be stuck on the active tab. They cannot reach other tabs. If they use virtual cursor to \"click\" another tab, it works \u2014 but this is not the standard keyboard workflow.\n\n**Keyboard-only user experience:** Tab into the component \u2192 land on active tab \u2192 cannot move to any other tab \u2192 Tab out \u2192 land in the active panel. **The component is effectively read-only for keyboard users.** They cannot switch tabs. This is the most severe finding.\n\n**Low vision user experience:** Focus indicator is visible (3px outline, 5.5:1 contrast). Active tab has three visual indicators (color, border, font-weight). Touch targets are adequate (12px padding + 16px font \u2248 40px+ height). No issues identified.\n\n**Cognitive user experience:** Clear labels, simple interaction, no confusing state. No issues identified.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REJECT** because the component has a **CRITICAL** finding: keyboard users cannot switch tabs. This is not a partial degradation \u2014 it is a complete access failure for the component's primary purpose. The ARIA attribute layer is correct (roles, states, roving tabindex, panel associations), which is why automated tools like axe-core will pass this component. But the keyboard interaction model \u2014 the thing that makes tabs *usable* by keyboard and screen reader users \u2014 is absent.\n\n**What would change the verdict to ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS:**\n- Implement ArrowLeft/ArrowRight/Home/End key handlers with focus movement (fixes Critical Finding #1)\n- Remove the redundant Enter/Space keydown handler (fixes Major Finding #2)\n- Add a heading to each panel (fixes Minor Finding #3)\n\n**What would change the verdict to ACCEPT:**\n- All of the above, plus\n- A Playwright keyboard test confirming ArrowRight moves focus and activates the next tab\n- A virtual-screen-reader test confirming the tab is announced with correct selected state after arrow key navigation\n\n**Recalibration notes:** No downgrades applied. The CRITICAL rating for Finding #1 survives the Realist Check: the realistic worst case is total loss of tab-switching for keyboard users, there is no workaround, and detection requires keyboard testing (not automated axe). The MAJOR rating for Finding #2 is maintained because it is evidence of an incomplete interaction model design, not just a code style issue.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. **Is this component used in a context where tabs are the only navigation mechanism?** If so, the CRITICAL finding escalates from \"component is inaccessible\" to \"section of the site is inaccessible.\" If tabs are supplementary (e.g., one of several ways to access the same content), the impact is lower but still CRITICAL for the component.\n\n2. **Are there any parent-level keyboard handlers** (e.g., a `useEffect` that adds a document-level keydown listener for arrow keys)? The code as presented has none, but if a parent component handles this, the finding would be invalidated. **Verify by checking the component's usage context.**\n\n3. **Is the `tabs` array guaranteed to be non-empty?** If `tabs` is empty, `tabs.findIndex()` in the keydown handler would return -1, and `tabs[newIndex]` would be undefined. Not an a11y issue per se, but a robustness gap that could cause a crash during keyboard interaction.\n\n4. **Does the page have a skip link?** Not visible in this component, but if the tabs are the first interactive element on the page, keyboard users have no way to skip past them. This is a page-level concern, not a component concern, but worth verifying.",
